WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.302 --> 00:00:03.603
-The Trump administration
is defying Congress

00:00:03.603 --> 00:00:05.137
over the Mueller report
as we get more information

00:00:05.137 --> 00:00:06.539
about the President's
tax returns.

00:00:06.539 --> 00:00:08.541
For more on this,
it's time for "A Closer Look."

00:00:08.541 --> 00:00:13.179
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:13.179 --> 00:00:15.749
After Democrats
won back the House in November

00:00:15.749 --> 00:00:17.016
and promised to investigate him,

00:00:17.016 --> 00:00:18.818
Trump threatened
to go to war with Congress,

00:00:18.818 --> 00:00:20.586
and now we're seeing
what that war looks like.

00:00:20.586 --> 00:00:22.254
Trump's fighting Congress
on every front,

00:00:22.254 --> 00:00:24.524
ordering former aides
to defy subpoenas,

00:00:24.524 --> 00:00:26.626
trying to block Robert Mueller
from testifying,

00:00:26.626 --> 00:00:29.429
suing banks to keep them from
releasing his bank documents,

00:00:29.429 --> 00:00:31.831
and refusing to open
Rudy Giuliani's coffin

00:00:31.831 --> 00:00:33.465
during the day.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:33.465 --> 00:00:35.068
"It's not because
he's a vampire!

00:00:35.068 --> 00:00:37.504
He just has sensitive eyes!"

00:00:37.504 --> 00:00:38.771
[ Laughter ]

00:00:38.771 --> 00:00:40.707
And then, of course,
there's William Barr,

00:00:40.707 --> 00:00:43.142
Trump's Attorney General
who lied about the contents

00:00:43.142 --> 00:00:45.378
of the Mueller report
before we all got to see it

00:00:45.378 --> 00:00:47.914
and then refused to show up
at a House hearing last week

00:00:47.914 --> 00:00:49.448
about his handling
of the report,

00:00:49.448 --> 00:00:51.917
leaving an empty chair in his
place, which was so weird.

00:00:51.917 --> 00:00:54.220
I mean, for one thing,
usually when you see someone

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:56.389
talking to an empty chair,
it's Clint Eastwood

00:00:56.389 --> 00:00:58.657
at the
Republican National Convention.

00:00:58.657 --> 00:01:03.396
Remember that insane thing that
happened before Donald Trump?

00:01:03.396 --> 00:01:06.699
An old man got up on national TV
and yelled at an empty chair

00:01:06.699 --> 00:01:08.635
while everyone in the audience
applauded and said,

00:01:08.635 --> 00:01:11.103
"Yeah!
You give that chair what for!"

00:01:11.103 --> 00:01:13.472
And that happened in 2012,
but somehow it took

00:01:13.472 --> 00:01:15.308
the emergence of Donald Trump
for us all to go,

00:01:15.308 --> 00:01:16.943
"Maybe Republicans are weird."

00:01:16.943 --> 00:01:18.010
[ Laughter ]

00:01:18.010 --> 00:01:19.478
Democrats also want Barr

00:01:19.478 --> 00:01:21.480
to turn over the full,
unredacted Mueller report

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:23.516
and all the underlying evidence
Mueller collected,

00:01:23.516 --> 00:01:25.785
and Barr has refused
to do that, as well.

00:01:25.785 --> 00:01:27.887
At a hearing
on a contempt resolution today,

00:01:27.887 --> 00:01:29.689
Republican Congressman
Jim Jordan asked

00:01:29.689 --> 00:01:31.624
the chairman of the committee,
Jerry Nadler,

00:01:31.624 --> 00:01:34.193
why he couldn't just wait
to hear from Mueller himself

00:01:34.193 --> 00:01:35.461
before getting the full report,

00:01:35.461 --> 00:01:37.863
and Nadler
had a very simple answer.

00:01:37.863 --> 00:01:40.633
-I would ask the chairman.
My understanding is,

00:01:40.633 --> 00:01:42.234
Mr. Mueller's
gonna be here next week.

00:01:42.234 --> 00:01:43.736
You're gonna get to ask the guy

00:01:43.736 --> 00:01:45.071
who wrote
the whole darn document.

00:01:45.071 --> 00:01:46.372
We're all gonna
ask him questions.

00:01:46.372 --> 00:01:48.174
Why don't you hold off
on this contempt

00:01:48.174 --> 00:01:49.942
until at least the guy
who wrote the thing,

00:01:49.942 --> 00:01:53.580
spent 22 months and $35 million
with a whole bunch

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:55.314
of Democratic lawyers
putting it together,

00:01:55.314 --> 00:01:56.816
why don't you wait
and ask him next week

00:01:56.816 --> 00:01:59.051
before we do
this contempt resolution?

00:01:59.051 --> 00:02:01.520
-I'll answer --
Well, essentially,

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:04.323
because it'll be useful
to read the material

00:02:04.323 --> 00:02:06.225
before we have him
in front of us.

00:02:06.225 --> 00:02:09.862
-Yeah, they want to
read it first.

00:02:09.862 --> 00:02:13.199
Republicans must have
the weirdest book clubs.

00:02:13.199 --> 00:02:15.535
"Okay. This week's book
is 'Great Gatsby.'

00:02:15.535 --> 00:02:17.070
What does everyone
think of the cover?"

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:18.505
[ Laughter ]

00:02:18.505 --> 00:02:20.573
And while Trump's administration
is trying to block the release

00:02:20.573 --> 00:02:22.242
of the full Mueller report,
they've also been

00:02:22.242 --> 00:02:23.475
in the middle of a stand-off
with Congress

00:02:23.475 --> 00:02:24.744
over Trump's tax returns.

00:02:24.744 --> 00:02:27.413
Democrats formally requested
Trump's tax returns,

00:02:27.413 --> 00:02:28.647
and this week,
the administration

00:02:28.647 --> 00:02:30.149
refused to hand them over.

00:02:30.149 --> 00:02:32.318
Last month, Trump even implied
that Mueller had looked at them

00:02:32.318 --> 00:02:33.819
and decided
there was nothing wrong.

00:02:33.819 --> 00:02:38.157
-I thought, after two years,
we'd be finished with it.

00:02:38.157 --> 00:02:41.627
No, now the House goes
and starts subpoenaing.

00:02:41.627 --> 00:02:43.796
They want to know
every deal I've ever done.

00:02:43.796 --> 00:02:47.734
Now, Mueller, I assume,
for $35 million,

00:02:47.734 --> 00:02:50.969
checked my taxes,
checked my financials,

00:02:50.969 --> 00:02:53.339
which are great by the way.
You know they're great.

00:02:53.339 --> 00:02:57.242
-If they're so great,
then just show them to us.

00:02:57.242 --> 00:02:59.478
You're the worst
poker player ever.

00:02:59.478 --> 00:03:01.947
He walks around
like he's holding four aces,

00:03:01.947 --> 00:03:03.650
but you just know
he has a pair of 2s

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:05.885
and that extra card
with the rules on it.

00:03:05.885 --> 00:03:07.720
[ Laughter ]

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:10.022
Also, I have no idea
whether Mueller --

00:03:10.022 --> 00:03:14.393
[ Cheers and applause ]
No idea...

00:03:14.393 --> 00:03:16.663
No idea whether Mueller
saw Trump's finances or not,

00:03:16.663 --> 00:03:19.765
but I feel like, if he had,
he would've told us about it.

00:03:19.765 --> 00:03:21.868
His report was 448 pages long.

00:03:21.868 --> 00:03:23.802
He included
every little detail he found.

00:03:23.802 --> 00:03:27.240
There's an entire section titled
"Trump's Hair and How It Works."

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:28.474
[ Laughter ]

00:03:28.474 --> 00:03:30.910
Now, Democrats had requested
the last six years

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:32.578
of Trump's returns,
but then, last night,

00:03:32.578 --> 00:03:33.846
we got
some blockbuster reporting

00:03:33.846 --> 00:03:35.681
from "The New York Times"
about Trump's tax returns

00:03:35.681 --> 00:03:38.484
from a different period in his
life, the '80s and '90s,

00:03:38.484 --> 00:03:40.686
when he was first cultivating
his celebrity status

00:03:40.686 --> 00:03:43.189
as a self-made success story.
And this will shock you --

00:03:43.189 --> 00:03:45.891
they were not,
as Trump claimed, great.

00:03:45.891 --> 00:03:47.693
-A brand-new report
from "The New York Times,"

00:03:47.693 --> 00:03:49.795
offering one of
the most detailed looks

00:03:49.795 --> 00:03:52.298
we have had
at Donald Trump's finances,

00:03:52.298 --> 00:03:55.301
during a decade-long portion
of his career.

00:03:55.301 --> 00:03:59.471
"The numbers show that, in 1985,
Mr. Trump reported losses

00:03:59.471 --> 00:04:03.876
of $46.1 million
from his core businesses,

00:04:03.876 --> 00:04:05.612
largely casinos, hotels,

00:04:05.612 --> 00:04:08.214
and retail space
in apartment buildings.

00:04:08.214 --> 00:04:10.750
They continued
to lose money every year,

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:15.487
totaling $1.17 billion
in losses for the decade.

00:04:15.487 --> 00:04:18.191
In fact, year after year,
Mr. Trump appears to have lost

00:04:18.191 --> 00:04:22.928
more money than nearly any other
individual American taxpayer.

00:04:22.928 --> 00:04:26.165
-He lost more
than a billion dollars.

00:04:26.165 --> 00:04:28.868
I can't even imag--
If my 401(k) goes down

00:04:28.868 --> 00:04:31.137
half a percent,
I need to take three Xanax.

00:04:31.137 --> 00:04:32.271
[ Laughter ]

00:04:32.271 --> 00:04:34.406
If I turn on CNN
and see a red arrow

00:04:34.406 --> 00:04:36.909
in the bottom right-hand corner,
I frantically call my broker

00:04:36.909 --> 00:04:38.378
and tell him
to take everything out.

00:04:38.378 --> 00:04:40.213
And my broker says,
"Take it out of what?

00:04:40.213 --> 00:04:41.814
You have all your money
in a checking account,"

00:04:41.814 --> 00:04:44.350
and then I say, "Is that bad?"
and he says, "Yes.

00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:46.018
And, also,
we've been over this --

00:04:46.018 --> 00:04:47.386
I'm not your broker.
I'm an actor.

00:04:47.386 --> 00:04:48.755
How did you get this number?"

00:04:48.755 --> 00:04:49.956
[ Laughter ]

00:04:49.956 --> 00:04:52.291
Now, we already knew
that Trump was a liar

00:04:52.291 --> 00:04:53.859
and that Trump was a cheat,

00:04:53.859 --> 00:04:56.328
who had a series of spectacular
business failures,

00:04:56.328 --> 00:04:58.363
but these returns
are from earlier in his life,

00:04:58.363 --> 00:04:59.632
in the '80s and '90s,

00:04:59.632 --> 00:05:02.134
when he was touting himself
as a business genius

00:05:02.134 --> 00:05:04.102
and one of the most
successful men in America.

00:05:04.102 --> 00:05:07.339
I mean, in 1987, while he was
losing all of this money,

00:05:07.339 --> 00:05:09.308
he literally wrote
"The Art of the Deal."

00:05:09.308 --> 00:05:11.944
Based on how bad he is at deals,

00:05:11.944 --> 00:05:13.813
I'm surprised
the cost of that book wasn't,

00:05:13.813 --> 00:05:15.782
"I pay you $20 if you take one."

00:05:15.782 --> 00:05:17.115
[ Laughter ]

00:05:17.115 --> 00:05:18.918
During this 10-year period,
Trump was actively

00:05:18.918 --> 00:05:21.387
building the myth
that he was a self-made success,

00:05:21.387 --> 00:05:23.723
while in reality,
he was a massive failure

00:05:23.723 --> 00:05:25.991
who lost more money than any
other American taxpayer.

00:05:25.991 --> 00:05:28.594
For example, you might remember
that, in the '80s and '90s,

00:05:28.594 --> 00:05:31.230
Trump pretended
to be a spokesman for himself,

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:33.165
named John Barron,
who would call

00:05:33.165 --> 00:05:36.168
various media outlets
and brag about Trump's success.

00:05:36.168 --> 00:05:38.437
For example,
here is Donald Trump

00:05:38.437 --> 00:05:42.808
calling a reporter at "Forbes,"
pretending to be John Barron.

00:05:42.808 --> 00:05:44.343
Specifically, he was trying
to convince the reporter

00:05:44.343 --> 00:05:46.178
that assets that belonged
to his father, Fred Trump,

00:05:46.178 --> 00:05:47.312
were actually his now.

00:05:47.312 --> 00:05:48.981
And while he was at it,
he also bragged

00:05:48.981 --> 00:05:51.550
about how close he was
with his father.

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:53.085
-Okay.
What's your first name,then?

00:05:53.085 --> 00:05:55.988
-John.
John Barron.

00:05:55.988 --> 00:05:58.724
Let me tell you
what the deal is,

00:05:58.724 --> 00:06:01.127
just so you understand.
-Okay, Mr. Trump.

00:06:01.127 --> 00:06:02.528
-First of all,
most of the assets

00:06:02.528 --> 00:06:04.130
have been consolidated
to Mr. Trump,

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:05.698
you know, because
you have down Fred Trump.

00:06:05.698 --> 00:06:07.967
But I think you can
really use Donald Trump now,

00:06:07.967 --> 00:06:09.969
and you can just consolidate it.

00:06:09.969 --> 00:06:12.137
-Does Fred Trump
make many management decisions?

00:06:12.137 --> 00:06:15.374
-Oh, yeah, sure, he's the --
he's the chairman.

00:06:15.374 --> 00:06:19.011
Donald's the president.
And Fred Trump is active.

00:06:19.011 --> 00:06:22.514
He's an excellent guy,
and you know how --

00:06:22.514 --> 00:06:24.116
They're very close,
their relationship.

00:06:24.116 --> 00:06:26.318
The two are, as you know,
very close, as you've heard

00:06:26.318 --> 00:06:28.888
or know,
or perhaps you don't know.

00:06:28.888 --> 00:06:31.724
-He used a fake name
to call a reporter

00:06:31.724 --> 00:06:33.692
and brag about
how much his father loves him.

00:06:33.692 --> 00:06:37.296
Man, I would say
the dude needs to therapy,

00:06:37.296 --> 00:06:39.165
but I don't think
he can afford it.

00:06:39.165 --> 00:06:41.100
[ Laughter ]
Maybe -- Maybe --

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:43.970
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:43.970 --> 00:06:47.640
Maybe he can just use a fake
name and be his own therapist.

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:49.942
"And how does that
make you feel, Donald?"

00:06:49.942 --> 00:06:52.411
"I feel smart, Dr. Barron."
[ Laughter ]

00:06:52.411 --> 00:06:55.648
"You are smart, Donald.
That'll be $200."

00:06:55.648 --> 00:06:58.584
"Uh..."
[ Laughter ]

00:06:58.584 --> 00:07:01.820
"Can I owe you?"

00:07:01.820 --> 00:07:03.755
So, the "Times" story
covers a period of 10 years,

00:07:03.755 --> 00:07:05.958
during which Trump
lost over a billion dollars,

00:07:05.958 --> 00:07:08.460
and yet throughout that period,
he was actively cultivating

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:10.796
his celebrity persona
as a self-made business titan.

00:07:10.796 --> 00:07:12.564
He told "Newsweek" in 1987,

00:07:12.564 --> 00:07:14.433
"There's no one my age
who has accomplished more,"

00:07:14.433 --> 00:07:16.902
but during that time,
there were also fleeting moments

00:07:16.902 --> 00:07:18.671
where Trump could barely hide
that he was lying.

00:07:18.671 --> 00:07:20.506
In fact, in an interview
that same year,

00:07:20.506 --> 00:07:22.408
on the first iteration
of this show,

00:07:22.408 --> 00:07:24.510
David Letterman tried to get
Trump to reveal his net worth.

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:26.945
Now keep in mind,
Trump has spent his whole life

00:07:26.945 --> 00:07:28.481
bragging about
how much he's worth.

00:07:28.481 --> 00:07:31.149
When he first announced his
presidential campaign in 2015,

00:07:31.149 --> 00:07:34.086
he literally said the words,
"I'm really rich."

00:07:34.086 --> 00:07:35.621
[ Laughter ]
And his campaign issued

00:07:35.621 --> 00:07:36.955
a press release that said,

00:07:36.955 --> 00:07:40.293
"Mr. Trump's net worth
is in excess of $10 billion."

00:07:40.293 --> 00:07:43.028
And you know it's true
'cause it's in all caps.

00:07:43.028 --> 00:07:45.364
It's like when you get an e-mail
with the subject line

00:07:45.364 --> 00:07:48.401
"Erectile dysfunction medicine,"
you're suspicious.

00:07:48.401 --> 00:07:50.570
But when it says,
"Free boner pills"...

00:07:50.570 --> 00:07:54.706
[ Laughter ]
...you know it's legit.

00:07:54.706 --> 00:07:57.076
And yet, in 1987, the year he
published "The Art of the Deal"

00:07:57.076 --> 00:07:59.579
and cast himself
as a self-made business genius,

00:07:59.579 --> 00:08:01.113
his tone was very different.

00:08:01.113 --> 00:08:02.481
When Letterman
asked him the question,

00:08:02.481 --> 00:08:04.817
you could tell Trump,
who has openly bragged

00:08:04.817 --> 00:08:06.886
throughout his life
about how much money he has,

00:08:06.886 --> 00:08:08.587
did not want to answer.

00:08:08.587 --> 00:08:10.189
-How much are you worth
right now,

00:08:10.189 --> 00:08:11.757
as you're sitting right here?

00:08:11.757 --> 00:08:13.993
[ Laughter ]

00:08:13.993 --> 00:08:15.461
-Zero idea, David.

00:08:15.461 --> 00:08:17.596
It depends on what's happening
with the world and the market.

00:08:17.596 --> 00:08:18.731
-I think this
is the kind of thing

00:08:18.731 --> 00:08:20.800
that people
love to fantasize about.

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:24.403
Because you are, unquestionably,
one of the most successful men

00:08:24.403 --> 00:08:25.604
in the country,
if not the world,

00:08:25.604 --> 00:08:26.838
and also one of the wealthiest.

00:08:26.838 --> 00:08:30.176
So, give us a figure
that we might ponder here.

00:08:30.176 --> 00:08:31.743
[ Laughter ]

00:08:31.743 --> 00:08:33.078
-You'll never
get it out of me, David.

00:08:33.078 --> 00:08:34.580
You'll never get it out of me.

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:36.214
-You won't put a figure?
Just an imaginary figure?

00:08:36.214 --> 00:08:38.183
-Never have, never would.

00:08:38.183 --> 00:08:39.952
-A billion. You're worth
a billion dollars?

00:08:39.952 --> 00:08:41.087
[ Laughter ]
-Perhaps.

00:08:41.087 --> 00:08:42.721
-Perhaps.

00:08:42.721 --> 00:08:45.324
-I mean, look how young he was.

00:08:45.324 --> 00:08:46.692
What happened to you, man?

00:08:46.692 --> 00:08:48.794
I mean, you actually used to
look like a normal person.

00:08:48.794 --> 00:08:51.964
How did that guy
turn into this guy?

00:08:51.964 --> 00:08:53.232
[ Laughter ]

00:08:53.232 --> 00:08:55.534
I mean, that looks like
the before-and-after

00:08:55.534 --> 00:08:57.435
in one of those anti-drug PSAs.

00:08:57.435 --> 00:08:58.771
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:08:58.771 --> 00:09:00.906
And, of course --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:09:00.906 --> 00:09:03.576
Look, Trump --

00:09:03.576 --> 00:09:05.710
Trump was never
a successful businessman.

00:09:05.710 --> 00:09:07.947
He just played one on TV.

00:09:07.947 --> 00:09:10.516
Expecting Donald Trump
to exhibit business acumen

00:09:10.516 --> 00:09:13.352
is like expecting George Clooney
to do an arterial bypass.

00:09:13.352 --> 00:09:14.520
[ Laughter ]

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:15.955
But this isn't
just a story about Trump.

00:09:15.955 --> 00:09:17.456
We already knew
he was a con artist.

00:09:17.456 --> 00:09:18.624
It's also a story
about the thing

00:09:18.624 --> 00:09:20.793
Trump cynically
claimed to fix,

00:09:20.793 --> 00:09:22.795
the thing he actually benefited
from his entire life,

00:09:22.795 --> 00:09:23.962
the rigged system.

00:09:23.962 --> 00:09:26.365
Most regular people
are one layoff

00:09:26.365 --> 00:09:28.900
or medical emergency away
from a financial crisis,

00:09:28.900 --> 00:09:32.338
but this guy lost
a billion dollars over 10 years

00:09:32.338 --> 00:09:33.673
and he ended up fine --

00:09:33.673 --> 00:09:35.107
except for the fact
that he went from looking

00:09:35.107 --> 00:09:36.408
like the wolf of Wall Street

00:09:36.408 --> 00:09:38.511
to a guy getting chased
by a pack of wolves.

00:09:38.511 --> 00:09:39.679
[ Laughter ]

00:09:39.679 --> 00:09:41.580
Trump is defying Congress
and subpoenas,

00:09:41.580 --> 00:09:43.048
and, once again,
we have more proof

00:09:43.048 --> 00:09:45.250
that he's one of the world's
most prolific con artists.

00:09:45.250 --> 00:09:48.354
Congress is exercising its duty
to investigate the President

00:09:48.354 --> 00:09:50.756
by asking for documents,
and Trump is just saying...

00:09:50.756 --> 00:09:52.191
-No!

00:09:52.191 --> 00:09:56.261
